.TH CCOL_SET_THREAD_NAME 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_set_thread_name \- rename the calling thread and make its log records carry the new name
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_set_thread_name(const char *thread_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Renames the calling thread, with prctl(PR_SET_NAME) on Linux and
pthread_setname_np(3) on FreeBSD and macOS.  The next record that the
thread writes, through any logger, carries the new name in the thread
group of its proc field.

Every record names the process and the thread that wrote it.  Each
thread reads its process ID, thread ID and thread name once, the first
time that it logs, and keeps them for the rest of its life, so a record
costs no system call for them.  This function updates the name that the
thread keeps.  A child that fork(2) makes reads all three again.

The Linux kernel stores at most 15 bytes of a thread name, so the
function keeps the first 15 bytes of thread_name on every platform; the
records and tools such as ps(1) and top(1) then show the same name.

The function needs no logger, so a thread can name itself before any
logger exists.  It affects only the calling thread.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
thread_name  The new name.  It must not be NULL or empty.  Only its
             first 15 bytes are used.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The thread has the new name, and its records
                    carry it.
ccol_invalid_args   thread_name is NULL or empty.  Nothing changes.
ccol_not_permitted  The system refused the rename, or the platform is
                    not Linux, FreeBSD or macOS.  Nothing changes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A thread renamed by any other means after its first record, such as
pthread_setname_np(3) or prctl(PR_SET_NAME), keeps its old name in its
records, so use this function to rename a thread that logs.
Thread-safe.  Each thread renames only itself.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
static void *worker(void *arg) {
    clog lg = *(clog *)arg;
    if (ccol_set_thread_name("ingest-worker") != ccol_success)
        return NULL;
    clog_info(lg, "started");
    /* proc=myapp(1234):ingest-worker(1240) */
    return NULL;
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR prctl (2)
